	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105021400421&doi=10.1145%2F3736181.3747124&partnerID=40&md5=09e6841bcb63a856378536b6e865e548},
	affiliations = {University of Cambridge, Cambridge, United Kingdom; University of Cape Coast Ghana, Cape Coast, Ghana},
	abstract = {Artificial intelligence (AI)-based technologies are increasingly permeating critical sectors in society. The public release of large language models has increased interest in the potential of AI and its implications for education. Given the perceived importance of AI literacy, some countries, including Ghana, are now introducing AI into the school curriculum. However, a critical question arises: do teachers possess the necessary subject matter knowledge and pedagogical strategies to effectively teach about AI in schools? This paper describes a qualitative study that involved ten computing teachers in junior high schools (ages 12-16) in Ghana, where AI has been part of the curriculum since 2021. The study explores emerging components of teachers' AI pedagogical content knowledge (PCK) in schools using a two-dimensional PCK model for computer science in K-12 educational settings. Our findings suggest several components of the teachers' PCK, including the use of learner-centered approaches such as discussion, group learning and research work by students. Our findings also highlight neural networks as a potentially difficult area of AI to learn in school. Teachers, although AI novices, revealed some motivation and positive attitudes toward teaching about AI in school. This paper contributes to the ongoing discourse on teachers' readiness to teach AI in schools. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
