	affiliations = {Københavns Universitet, Copenhagen Center for Social Data Science, Copenhagen, Denmark; DWG Datenwissenschaftliche Gesellschaft Berlin, Berlin, Germany; Elinkeinoelämän Tutkimuslaitos, Helsinki, Finland; Oxford Social Sciences Division, Oxford, United Kingdom; Einstein Center Digital Future, Berlin, Germany; San Francisco, San Francisco, United States; University College London, Department of Computer Science, London, United Kingdom; University of Cambridge, Cambridge, United Kingdom; Complexity Science Hub Vienna, Vienna, Austria},
	abstract = {We examine how ChatGPT has changed the demand for freelancers in jobs where generative AI tools can act as substitutes or complements to human labor. Using BERTopic we partition job postings from a leading online freelancing platform into 116 fine-grained skill clusters and with GPT-4o we classify them as substitutable, complementary or unaffected by LLMs. Our analysis reveals that labor demand increased after the launch of ChatGPT, but only in skill clusters that were complementary to or unaffected by the AI tool. In contrast, demand for substitutable skills, such as writing and translation, decreased by 20–50% relative to the counterfactual trend, with the sharpest decline observed for short-term (1-3 week) jobs. Within complementary skill clusters, the results are mixed: demand for machine learning programming grew by 24%, and demand for AI-powered chatbot development nearly tripled, while demand for novice workers declined in general. This result suggests a shift toward more specialized expertise for freelancers rather than uniform growth across all complementary areas. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Automation and employment; Generative AI technologies; Labor market implications of AI; Large language models; Online labor markets; Technological transition},
