	affiliations = {Instituto Superior de Engenharia do Porto, Porto, Portugal; Universidade do Porto, Porto, Portugal; Institute for Systems and Computer Engineering, Technology and Science, Porto, Portugal},
	abstract = {The trend for an increasingly ubiquitous and cyber-physical world has been leveraging the use and importance of microcontrollers ((Formula presented.) C) to unprecedented levels. Therefore, microcontroller programming ((Formula presented.) CP) becomes a paramount skill for electrical and computer engineering students. However, (Formula presented.) CP poses significant challenges for undergraduate students, given the need to master low-level programming languages and several algorithmic strategies that are not usual in “generic” programming. Moreover, (Formula presented.) CP can be time-consuming and complex even when using high-level languages. This article samples the current state of (Formula presented.) CP education in Portugal and unveils the potential support of natural language processing (NLP) tools (such as chatGPT). Our analysis of (Formula presented.) CP curricular units from seven representative Portuguese engineering schools highlights a predominant use of AVR 8-bit (Formula presented.) C and project-based learning. While NLP tools emerge as strong candidates as students' (Formula presented.) C companion, their application and impact on the learning process and outcomes deserve to be understood. This study compares the most prominent NLP tools, analyzing their benefits and drawbacks for (Formula presented.) CP education, building on both hands-on tests and literature reviews. By providing automatic code generation and explanation of concepts, NLP tools can assist students in their learning process, allowing them to focus on software design and real-world tasks that the (Formula presented.) C is designed to handle, rather than on low-level coding. We also analyzed the specific impact of chatGTP in the context of a (Formula presented.) CP course at ISEP, confirming most of our expectations, but with a few curiosities. Overall, this work establishes the foundations for future research on the effective integration of NLP tools in (Formula presented.) CP courses. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {artificial intelligence; AVR microcontrollers; chatbot; chatGPT; code generation; CodeGeeX; engineering education; Github Copilot; large language model (LLM); learning outcomes; microcontroller programming; natural language processing (NLP); notion; NotionAI; OpenAI; programming paradigms; project-based learning; software design},
